{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>The ox shall be surely stoned.</b> Rashi explained: from the implication of what is said, 'the ox shall be surely stoned' — I would not know that it may not be eaten, for it is a nevelah, and nevelah is forbidden in eating. Rather, it teaches that if one slaughtered it after its verdict was completed, it is forbidden in eating. And if you ask: I will say 'it shall not be eaten' refers to a ben peku'ah, which is permitted through the slaughter of its mother, and this verse comes to say that if that ben peku'ah gored and was stoned, even though it is not a nevelah, it should be forbidden — the answer is: since a ben peku'ah is permitted without slaughter, if so it is as though it was slaughtered before its verdict was completed, and we hold that it is permitted through slaughter."], :book :exodus, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:03:48.110081449Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}